The Security Council,
Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity and national unity of all Member States and to the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,
Reaffirming that terrorism in all its forms and manifestations constitutes one of the most serious threats to international peace and security, and that all terrorist acts are criminal and unjustifiable, regardless of their motivation, wherever, whenever and by whomsoever committed,
Recalling its resolutions 1267 (1999), 1373 (2001), 1989 (2011), 2178 (2014), 2253 (2015), 2322 (2016), 2396 (2017), 2462 (2019), 2610 (2021), 2665 (2022), 2716 (2023), 2734 (2024), 2763 (2024) and 2799 (2025), and all other relevant resolutions,
Condemning in the strongest terms the terrorist attack in Palmyra, Syrian Arab Republic, on 13 December 2025, which killed two United States service members and one United States civilian interpreter and injured others, and condemning in the strongest terms the terrorist attacks in Bondi Beach, Australia, in December 2025 and in Maiduguri, Borno State, Nigeria, on 24 December 2025, as well as all other recent terrorist attacks and disrupted terrorist plots,
Expressing grave concern at the continuing threat posed by ISIL (Da’esh), Al-Qaida and their affiliated groups, including Boko Haram and other ISIL-affiliated groups, and by foreign terrorist fighters, and at the risk of the resurgence of terrorist networks in areas affected by conflict and instability,
Reaffirming the inherent right of individual or collective self-defence in accordance with Article 51 of the Charter, and stressing that all measures taken to counter terrorism must be consistent with international law, including the Charter, international human rights law, international refugee law and international humanitarian law,
Recalling the commitments of the Syrian Arab Republic to counter terrorism, including ISIL (Da’esh), Al-Qaida and foreign terrorist fighters; to secure its borders; to prevent the resurgence of terrorist networks; and to cooperate with the relevant Security Council sanctions mechanisms, and recalling in this regard resolution 2799 (2025),
Stressing the importance of full implementation of the ISIL (Da’esh) and Al-Qaida sanctions regime, including the timely submission of listing requests and the exchange of information through the 1267 Committee and its Monitoring Team,
Reaffirming that terrorism cannot and should not be associated with any religion, nationality, civilization or ethnic group, and underscoring the need to address conditions conducive to terrorism, including through the protection of civilians and the promotion of accountability,
Stressing that sexual and gender-based violence, including sexual slavery, abduction, forced marriage and rape, when used by terrorist groups as a tactic, is a serious violation of international law and must be addressed through accountability and support for survivors,
Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Condemns in the strongest terms the terrorist attack in Palmyra, Syrian Arab Republic, on 13 December 2025, and all other recent terrorist attacks, including those in Bondi Beach, Australia, and Maiduguri, Nigeria, and expresses its deepest condolences to the families of the victims and to the Governments and peoples affected;
2. Reaffirms that terrorism in all its forms and manifestations constitutes one of the most serious threats to international peace and security, and demands that all terrorist groups, including ISIL (Da’esh), Al-Qaida, Boko Haram and their affiliated groups, cease immediately all acts of violence, disarm, and prevent the recruitment, financing, training and movement of foreign terrorist fighters;
3. Calls upon all Member States to fully implement their obligations under the relevant Security Council resolutions, including by preventing the travel of terrorists and foreign terrorist fighters, preventing and suppressing terrorist financing, disrupting the supply of weapons to terrorist groups, strengthening border security and sharing information, in accordance with international law;
4. Calls upon the Syrian Arab Republic to continue to take decisive measures to counter terrorism, including ISIL (Da’esh), Al-Qaida and foreign terrorist fighters; to secure its borders; to prevent the resurgence of terrorist networks; to protect human rights and the safety and security of all Syrians regardless of ethnicity or religion; and to cooperate fully with the 1267 Committee, its Monitoring Team and the Ombudsperson, and recalls in this regard the expectations set out in resolution 2799 (2025);
5. Welcomes the efforts of the Global Coalition against ISIL (Da’esh), regional organizations and Member States to support the fight against ISIL (Da’esh), Al-Qaida and their affiliated groups, consistent with international law and the sovereignty, territorial integrity and political independence of States, and encourages continued coordination to prevent the resurgence of terrorism in Syria and the wider region;
6. Urges Member States to strengthen their cooperation in the areas of border security, law enforcement, criminal justice, countering terrorist financing, countering the use of the Internet and social media for terrorist purposes, and preventing radicalization to terrorism, including through public-private partnerships and the work of relevant United Nations bodies;
7. Calls upon all Member States to bring perpetrators, organizers, financiers and sponsors of terrorist acts to justice, and stresses the importance of accountability for all terrorist acts, including those involving sexual and gender-based violence;
8. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council within 90 days on the threat posed by ISIL (Da’esh), Al-Qaida and their affiliated groups in Syria, on the implementation of resolution 2799 (2025), and on cooperation by the Syrian Arab Republic with the relevant Security Council sanctions mechanisms, and to brief the Council on that report;
9. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

The Security Council,
Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity and national unity of all Member States and to the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,
Reaffirming that terrorism in all its forms and manifestations constitutes one of the most serious threats to international peace and security, and that all terrorist acts are criminal and unjustifiable, regardless of their motivation, wherever, whenever and by whomsoever committed,
