Condemning in the strongest terms the terrorist attack in Palmyra, Syrian Arab Republic, on 13 December 2025, which killed two United States service members and one United States civilian interpreter and injured others, and condemning in the strongest terms the terrorist attacks in Bondi Beach, Australia, in December 2025 and in Maiduguri, Borno State, Nigeria, on 24 December 2025, as well as all other recent terrorist attacks and disrupted terrorist plots,
Expressing grave concern at the continuing threat posed by ISIL (Da’esh), Al-Qaida and their affiliated groups, including Boko Haram and other ISIL-affiliated groups, and by foreign terrorist fighters, and at the risk of the resurgence of terrorist networks in areas affected by conflict and instability,
Stressing that counter-terrorism measures, including sanctions, should be implemented in accordance with international law and should not impede impartial humanitarian action carried out by impartial humanitarian actors in accordance with humanitarian principles,
Reaffirming the inherent right of individual or collective self-defence in accordance with Article 51 of the Charter, and stressing that all measures taken to counter terrorism must be consistent with international law, including the Charter, international human rights law, international refugee law and international humanitarian law,
Recalling the commitments of the Syrian Arab Republic to counter terrorism, including ISIL (Da’esh), Al-Qaida and foreign terrorist fighters; to secure its borders; to prevent the resurgence of terrorist networks; and to cooperate with the relevant Security Council sanctions mechanisms, and recalling in this regard resolution 2799 (2025),
Reaffirming the importance of a Syrian-owned and Syrian-led political process, in line with the principles of resolution 2254 (2015), as essential to lasting stability and to preventing the resurgence of terrorism, and stressing the need for a comprehensive approach addressing conditions conducive to terrorism,
Stressing the importance of full implementation of the ISIL (Da’esh) and Al-Qaida sanctions regime, including the timely submission of listing requests and the exchange of information through the 1267 Committee and its Monitoring Team,
Stressing that any further adjustment of the ISIL (Da’esh) and Al-Qaida sanctions regime related to Syria must be based on a comprehensive assessment of the counter-terrorism and security situation in Syria and the legitimate concerns of all parties,
Reaffirming that terrorism cannot and should not be associated with any religion, nationality, civilization or ethnic group, condemning all incitement to terrorism and acts of religious intolerance, including Islamophobia, and underscoring the need to address conditions conducive to terrorism, including through the protection of civilians and the promotion of accountability,
Stressing that sexual and gender-based violence, including sexual violence, when used by terrorist groups as a tactic, including by ISIL (Da’esh) against Yazidis, Christians and other religious and ethnic minorities, is a serious violation of international law and must be addressed through accountability, including the collection and preservation of evidence, and support for survivors,
Expressing grave concern at the recruitment, abduction and use of children by terrorist groups, and stressing that children associated with terrorist groups should be treated primarily as victims, consistent with international law,
Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Condemns in the strongest terms the terrorist attack in Palmyra, Syrian Arab Republic, on 13 December 2025, and all other recent terrorist attacks, including those in Bondi Beach, Australia, and Maiduguri, Nigeria, and expresses its deepest condolences to the families of the victims and to the Governments and peoples affected;
2. Reaffirms that terrorism in all its forms and manifestations constitutes one of the most serious threats to international peace and security, and demands that all terrorist groups, including ISIL (Da’esh), Al-Qaida, the East Turkestan Islamic Movement (ETIM/Turkistan Islamic Party), Al-Shabaab, Boko Haram and their affiliated groups, cease immediately all acts of violence, disarm, and prevent the recruitment, financing, training and movement of foreign terrorist fighters;
3. Calls upon all Member States to fully implement their obligations under the relevant Security Council resolutions, including by preventing the travel of terrorists and foreign terrorist fighters; preventing and suppressing terrorist financing; disrupting the supply of weapons to terrorist groups; strengthening border security in accordance with international law, including international human rights law, international refugee law and international humanitarian law and respecting the principle of non-refoulement; and sharing information, in accordance with international law; and calls upon Member States and relevant international bodies to provide technical assistance and capacity-building to developing countries, particularly in Africa, upon request, to implement these measures, including compliance with Financial Action Task Force standards on combating the financing of terrorism and anti-money laundering;
4. Calls upon the Syrian Arab Republic to continue to take decisive measures to counter terrorism, including ISIL (Da’esh), Al-Qaida, foreign terrorist fighters and all other terrorist groups designated by the Security Council, without double standards or politicization; to secure its borders; to prevent the resurgence of terrorist networks; to protect human rights and the safety and security of all Syrians regardless of ethnicity or religion, and to ensure full, safe, rapid and unhindered humanitarian access, consistent with international humanitarian law; and to cooperate fully with the 1267 Committee, its Monitoring Team and the Ombudsperson, and recalls in this regard the expectations set out in resolution 2799 (2025);
5. Takes note of the efforts of Member States and regional organizations, including the African Union, the Economic Community of West African States, the Multinational Joint Task Force and the Accra Initiative, to support the fight against ISIL (Da’esh), Al-Qaida and their affiliated groups, in full respect of the sovereignty, territorial integrity and political independence of States and, where applicable, with the consent of the host State, and, in the case of Syria, in coordination with the Syrian Arab Republic, and encourages continued coordination to prevent the resurgence of terrorism in Syria and the wider region, and calls for enhanced international support, including through predictable, adequate and sustainable financing, including voluntary contributions, for African-led counter-terrorism efforts and, as appropriate, for African Union-led peace support operations authorized by the Security Council, and for capacity-building of national security and justice institutions in States affected by terrorism, including Somalia, in accordance with international law and the sovereignty of the States concerned;
6. Urges Member States to strengthen their cooperation in the areas of border security, law enforcement, criminal justice, countering terrorist financing, including through virtual assets and other new payment methods, countering the use of the Internet and social media for terrorist purposes, consistent with international law, including international human rights law, international refugee law, international humanitarian law, freedom of expression and privacy, and preventing radicalization to terrorism and addressing conditions conducive to terrorism, including through education, youth engagement, socio-economic development, public-private partnerships, dialogue among civilizations, religions and cultures, and the work of relevant United Nations bodies, while ensuring that counter-terrorism measures do not target any religion or community, and to provide technical assistance and capacity-building to developing countries, upon request, including in the areas of maritime and port security;
7. Calls upon all Member States to bring perpetrators, organizers, financiers and sponsors of terrorist acts to justice, and stresses the importance of accountability for all terrorist acts, including those involving sexual and gender-based violence, including sexual violence; calls for survivors, in particular women and children, to have access to justice, reparations and medical and psychosocial support; calls for support and assistance to victims and survivors of terrorism; and calls for the humane treatment, rehabilitation and reintegration of children associated with terrorist groups, including through repatriation where appropriate and in line with the best interests of the child;
8. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council within 90 days on the threat posed by ISIL (Da’esh), Al-Qaida and their affiliated groups, including Boko Haram, in Syria and the wider region, including the Sahel and Lake Chad Basin, on the implementation of resolution 2799 (2025), and on cooperation by all Member States, including the Syrian Arab Republic, with the relevant Security Council sanctions mechanisms, on the basis of information provided by Member States and the 1267 Monitoring Team, including on compliance with international law, in particular international human rights law, international refugee law and international humanitarian law, and on the protection of civilians and any humanitarian impact of counter-terrorism measures, as well as on capacity-building needs identified by Member States, and to brief the Council on that report;
9. Stresses that all counter-terrorism measures taken pursuant to this resolution must be consistent with international law, including international humanitarian law and international human rights law, and must not impede the delivery of humanitarian assistance or the protection of civilians;
10. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

